<p rendition="#et2"><hi rendition="#i">Lat.</hi>: A soles occasu, non ortu, describe diem. (<hi rendition="#i">Binder I, 93; II, 15; Buchler, 143; Philippi, I, 44; Seybold, 40.</hi>) &#x2013; Diem vesper commendat. (<hi rendition="#i">Binder I, 319; II, 766; Seybold, 123.</hi>) &#x2013; Nescis quid serus vesper vehat. (<hi rendition="#i">Binder II, 2067; Erasm., 416; Philippi, II, 19; Schonheim, N, 16; Tappius, 140<hi rendition="#sup">a</hi>.</hi>) &#x2013; Pane laudato; nam quem tu saepe probaris una dies, qualis fuerit, ostendet, amicus. (<hi rendition="#i">Philippi, II, 81.</hi>) &#x2013; Vespere flet crebro, qui risit mane serena. (<hi rendition="#i">Philippi, II, 247.</hi>)</p>                <lb/>
<p rendition="#et2"><hi rendition="#i">Span.</hi>: Al fin loa la vida y a la noche loa el dia. (<hi rendition="#i">Masson, 236.</hi>)</p>                <lb/>
<p rendition="#et"><hi rendition="#larger">376 Man soll sich einen Tag vor seinem Tode zu Gott bekehren.</hi></p>                <lb/>
